                    text: "Soap, shampoo, detergent and dishwash are daily purchases, so volume is steady; the fight is over price and shelf space. GST on soap, shampoo and toothpaste fell from 18% to 5%. The cost side is crude: about 90% of **Jyothy**'s business uses crude-linked inputs, and LABSA, the main detergent chemical, rose 60–65% from January while plastic packaging rose 50–55% by early May. Rivals cut prices and added extra grammage for six quarters, so Jyothy realised only about 3% of price in Q1 against about 5% at HUL and Godrej Consumer (analysts' figures). **Honasa** is less exposed: it outsources manufacturing, earns a gross margin of about 70%, and spends about 32% of revenue on advertising and promotion instead. Channels are shifting fast: modern trade, e-commerce and quick commerce grew 25–30% at Jyothy, and 68% of Honasa's FY26 revenue was online. **My view: in this corner brand spending and distribution matter more than factories, and crude decides the margin in any given year.**",
